  "prediction": "**Risk Management in Federal Facilities: Challenges and Recommendations**\n\nThe Interagency Security Committee (ISC) has established a risk management process for federal facilities to ensure the security of these facilities. However, a recent review of four federal agencies (U.S. Customs and Border Protection, Federal Aviation Administration, Agricultural Research Service, and Forest Service) found that their assessment methodologies do not fully align with the ISC standard.\n\nThe review found that none of the four agencies' security assessment methodologies fully aligned with the ISC Standard, which requires agencies to consider all 33 undesirable events, assess threats, vulnerabilities, and consequences, and document deviations from the standard. The agencies' methodologies were also found to be inconsistent in their application, with some agencies not documenting their decisions or justifying their deviations from the standard.\n\nThe review also identified several management challenges faced by the agencies in conducting physical security assessments and monitoring results. These challenges included:\n\n*   Backlogs in conducting security assessments, with some agencies having not reassessed their facilities in over 3 years.\n*   Inadequate data collection and analysis to monitor the performance of physical security programs.\n*   Lack of a long-term assessment schedule with key milestones to ensure that higher-level facilities are reassessed at least once every 3 years.\n\nTo address these challenges, the review made several recommendations, including:\n\n*   The Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection should revise the assumptions used in the plan to address the assessment backlog to balance assessments with competing priorities.\n*   The Secretary of Transportation should direct the FAA Administrator to develop a plan that provides sufficient details on the activities needed and time frames within the date when FAA will implement an improved methodology.\n*   The Secretary of Agriculture should direct the Administrator of the Agricultural Research Service and the Chief of the Forest Service to implement and monitor a long-term assessment schedule with key milestones to ensure that higher-level facilities are reassessed at least once every 3 years.\n\nThe review also found that the agencies' physical security programs were not adequately aligned with the ISC standard, which could result in facilities having inadequate security or unnecessary use of resources. The review recommended that the agencies update their policies and procedures to require a methodology that adheres to the ISC standard and includes data collection and analysis requirements for monitoring the performance of physical security programs.\n\nOverall, the review found that the four federal agencies reviewed had significant challenges in conducting physical security assessments and monitoring results, and that their assessment methodologies did not fully align with the ISC standard. The review made several recommendations to address these challenges and improve the alignment of the agencies' physical security",
